  23. Performance Implications

  Micrometer counters and gauges use ConcurrentHashMap-backed concurrent data structures. Incrementing a counter is a single CAS operation — sub-microsecond. This is negligible relative to HTTP handler, JPA, and PostGIS query latency.

  The @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) listener is synchronous in the same thread as the commit. Adding a counter increment to AreaMetricsListener.onAreaEvent() adds nanoseconds to the post-commit path, which is not in the request response path (the HTTP response is returned before AFTER_COMMIT listeners run — the response is flushed before the commit callback fires). Correction: AFTER_COMMIT runs after the transaction commits but before the HTTP response is sent by the @Transactional interceptor. The latency impact is still nanoseconds and not a concern.

  The MetricsChannelInterceptor for WebSocket CONNECT/DISCONNECT runs in the STOMP inbound channel thread, separate from the HTTP request thread. Incrementing an AtomicInteger (backing the gauge) is nanoseconds.

  Conclusion: No meaningful performance impact.

